<Legal title="Privacy" description="What the Holdall project sees about you: the sign-in relay, this website and the update checks." updated="14 November 2026">
  <h2>In short</h2>
  <p>
    Holdall runs on your own computer or server. Your notes, tasks, calendars and files stay there. The Holdall project has no account system, no analytics and no
    advertising. This page lists the few moments where something from your Holdall reaches a service run by the project.
  </p>

  <h2>The sign-in relay (auth.holdall.site)</h2>
  <p>
    Google and Microsoft only let registered apps open their sign-in pages. So that you do not have to register your own app, the project runs a small relay at
    auth.holdall.site. It is used when you click <b>Connect Google Calendar</b> or <b>Connect Outlook</b> in a Holdall that has the relay turned on, which is the
    default.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      When you connect, the relay receives the address of your Holdall, sends you to Google or Microsoft, and receives the access granted to your calendar. It encrypts
      that access with a one-time key made by your Holdall for this sign-in, so it never shows readable in your browser history, and sends it straight back to your
      Holdall.
    </li>
    <li>
      About once an hour, while your calendar syncs, your Holdall asks the relay to renew the access. The relay passes the request to Google or Microsoft and returns the answer.
    </li>
    <li>The relay stores nothing: no database, no files, no copy of your access or of your calendar. It never reads your events.</li>
    <li>
      Its log only notes errors, with the provider name and the error code from the provider. Addresses are kept in memory for a few minutes to limit abuse, then
      forgotten.
    </li>
    <li>The relay is open source: its code is <a href={`${repo}/blob/main/scripts/relay.ts`}>scripts/relay.ts</a> in the Holdall repository.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Your calendar data goes from Google or Microsoft to your Holdall directly. Your Holdall stores the access encrypted, and only uses it to sync your calendar. You can
    remove it at any time from <b>Calendar, Accounts, Remove</b>, and revoke it at https://myaccount.google.com/permissions or https://account.live.com/consent/Manage.
  </p>
  <p>
    Holdall's use and transfer of information received from Google APIs adheres to the
    <a href="https://developers.google.com/terms/api-services-user-data-policy">Google API Services User Data Policy</a>, including the Limited Use requirements. The
    project does not sell, share or use this information for advertising, and no person at the project reads it.
  </p>
  <p>The admin of a Holdall can turn the relay off and use their own Google and Microsoft apps instead. Then nothing reaches the relay.</p>

  <h2>This website and the demo</h2>
  <p>
    holdall.site is hosted on GitHub Pages and the demo on Render, both behind Cloudflare. These services see your IP address and browser like any website. The project
    adds no cookies, analytics or trackers. A demo account and everything in it are deleted after one hour.
  </p>

  <h2>Update checks</h2>
  <p>
    A Holdall server asks GitHub once a day for the latest release, and the desktop app does it when it starts. Nothing about you or your data is sent. Server admins can
    turn it off with <code>UPDATE_CHECK=false</code>.
  </p>

  <h2>Contact</h2>
  <p>For any question about this page or to report a problem, open an issue at <a href={`${repo}/issues`}>github.com/Toutourina0110/holdall/issues</a>.</p>
</Legal>
